To achieve this, you'll need to use HTML and JavaScript to place an SVG icon next to your input field and make it clickable. Here's an example of how you can do this:

1. Create an HTML structure with an input field and an SVG icon.
2. Add an `onclick` event listener to the SVG icon to call the desired function when clicked.

Here's a sample implementation:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>SVG Icon Clickable Input</title>
    <style>
        .input-wrapper {
            position: relative;
            display: inline-block;
        }
        .input-icon {
            position: absolute;
            right: 10px;
            top: 50%;
            transform: translateY(-50%);
            cursor: pointer;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="input-wrapper">
        <input type="text" id="myInput" placeholder="Type something...">
        <svg class="input-icon" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" onclick="onInputClick()">
            <path d="M5 12H19M5 3v10m4 10V3m-4-10H5z" />
        </svg>
    </div>

    <script>
        function onInputClick() {
            // Your desired function to be called when the icon is clicked
            console.log("Input clicked");
            // You can add more logic here, for example:
            alert("Input field clicked!");
        }
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

Explanation:
- The `.input-wrapper` class is used to position the input field and the SVG icon relative to each other.
- The `.input-icon` class is used to position the SVG icon to the right side of the input field.
- The SVG icon is made clickable by adding an `onclick` event listener that calls the `onInputClick` function when clicked.
- The `onInputClick` function contains the logic you want to execute when the icon is clicked.

You can customize the SVG icon and the function called on click according to your requirements.

source_response:
To achieve this, you can use HTML and JavaScript to create a clickable SVG icon next to an input element and handle the `onClick` event for the icon. Here’s how you can do it:

1. **HTML**: Create an input element and an SVG icon.
2. **CSS**: Style the input and icon as needed.
3. **JavaScript**: Add an `onClick` event listener to the SVG icon to call a function.

Here's an example:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Input with SVG Icon</title>
    <style>
        .input-container {
            display: flex;
            align-items: center;
            margin-bottom: 10px;
        }

        input[type="text"] {
            flex: 1;
            padding: 5px;
            border: 1px solid #ccc;
            border-radius: 5px;
        }

        .icon {
            cursor: pointer;
            margin-left: 5px;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="input-container">
        <input type="text" id="myInput" placeholder="Type something">
        <svg class="icon" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" onClick="handleClick()">
            <path d="M21 21L15 15M17 2l3 3m-7 0l3 3m0-10L7 15"></path>
        </svg>
    </div>

    <script>
        function handleClick() {
            // Add your function logic here
            console.log("Icon clicked!");
        }
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Explanation:

1. **HTML**:
   - The `div` with the class `input-container` contains both the input field and the SVG icon.
   - The input field is styled to take up most of the available space in the container.
   - The SVG icon is placed next to the input field.

2. **CSS**:
   - The `input-container` uses `flex` to align the input and the icon horizontally.
   - The `input` element has padding and border styles for better appearance.
   - The `.icon` class is styled to have a `cursor: pointer` to indicate it's clickable and adds a margin to the right of the input.

3. **JavaScript**:
   - The `handleClick` function is defined to be called when the icon is clicked.
   - You can replace the `console.log("Icon clicked!");` with your desired function logic.

This setup ensures that the SVG icon is displayed next to the input field and is clickable, calling the `handleClick` function when clicked.

